From 9043bbb742e978c07d3b978ea8287a9cbe70b573 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Devandroid-bit <180217512+Devandroid-bit@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 21:36:27 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] f2fs: use unfair rwsem to avoid writer starvation

Backport the reader-unfair rwsem support to the Exynos9820 4.14
kernel, following upstream commit e4544b63a7ee ("f2fs: move f2fs to
use reader-unfair rwsems") and the config-gated rework that
introduced CONFIG_F2FS_UNFAIR_RWSEM.

All f2fs internal rw semaphores are converted to struct f2fs_rwsem, a
wrapper that is explicitly unfair to readers: readers only take the
internal rwsem through down_read_trylock() and otherwise sleep on a
dedicated wait queue that the writer wakes up on up_write(). This
prevents priority inversion where low-priority readers queue ahead of
a high-priority writer (e.g. the checkpoint or GC thread) and stall
foreground operations, which is a common source of jank under memory
pressure.

The sentry_lock and journal_rwsem are intentionally left as plain
rwsem, matching upstream. The new behaviour is gated behind
CONFIG_F2FS_UNFAIR_RWSEM, enabled in the Exynos9820 defconfig.

